[{:db/id 17592186229191 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c52ae607-3210-5388-a941-db6a09d117b2" "question-a0a5fd86-1340-5688-ba59-8ec4e8026e82"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228922 :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n\nII. $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n\nIII. $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c52ae607-3210-5388-a941-db6a09d117b2" :question/id #uuid "c52ae607-3210-5388-a941-db6a09d117b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334938" :question/problem "Given that $J(t)={\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${J}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${J}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${J}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)={\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{J}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(5x+t)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(5x+t)^{2}] & =2(5x+t)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(5x+t) \\\\  & =2(5x+t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2(5x+t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2(5x+t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(5x+t)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}5x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{1}^{3}+tx{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =20+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & =2(20+2t) \\\\  & =4(t+10).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a8abd96-4c9a-53da-b4f4-8bc3009cd780"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a8abd96-4c9a-53da-b4f4-8bc3009cd780" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a8abd96-4c9a-53da-b4f4-8bc3009cd780" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fba7ad86-3a5a-50af-8227-5ad1c1f14e0e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fba7ad86-3a5a-50af-8227-5ad1c1f14e0e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fba7ad86-3a5a-50af-8227-5ad1c1f14e0e" :answer/id #uuid "fba7ad86-3a5a-50af-8227-5ad1c1f14e0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40+4t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a0a5fd86-1340-5688-ba59-8ec4e8026e82" :question/id #uuid "a0a5fd86-1340-5688-ba59-8ec4e8026e82" :question/math-academy-id "q-352881" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{2t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{3}] & =3({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{e}^{2t}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+{e}^{2t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{4t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=6{e}^{2t}(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9450d3d5-ae63-5af3-bb4d-54253eb9dcc6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9450d3d5-ae63-5af3-bb4d-54253eb9dcc6" :answer-field/id #uuid "9450d3d5-ae63-5af3-bb4d-54253eb9dcc6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fccadc68-ba31-5689-a5be-fac6a54a34ea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fccadc68-ba31-5689-a5be-fac6a54a34ea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fccadc68-ba31-5689-a5be-fac6a54a34ea" :answer/id #uuid "fccadc68-ba31-5689-a5be-fac6a54a34ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}e^{2t}+e^{4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311549 :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229680 :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230184 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^{b} f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the integration variable and $t$ is a parameter. How do we compute $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} I}{\\mathrm{d} t}$?\n\nIf $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b] \\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R,$ then the function\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d} t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as **differentiating under the integral sign**.\n\nThe integral represents the total area under the curve $y = f(x,t).$ Changing the parameter $t$ alters the height of the curve at each $x,$ but the interval $[a,b]$ remains fixed.\nThus, the total change in area is obtained by summing (integrating) how each point changes.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} e^{-tx}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-tx}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}({e}^{-tx})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-x{e}^{-tx}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can then evaluate this integral using the usual methods. For the purposes of integration, we treat $t$ as a constant." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230220 :tutorial/content "Now, given that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle I(t)=\\int_{-1}^{1} (3x-t^2)^2\\,\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nlet's calculate $I'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$ by *differentiating under the integral sign*.\n\nRecall that if $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R,$ then the function\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute $I'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\int_{-1}^{1} (3x-t^2)^2\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\int_{-1}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(3x-t^2)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-{t}^{2})^{2}] & =2(3x-{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3x-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3x-{t}^{2})\\cdot (-2t) \\\\  & =-4t(3x-{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-4t(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4t{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1}-{t}^{2}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =0-2{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =-4t(-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229252 :activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
